course: dictionary
lesson: 3451
title: A place that makes things
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A factory is a big building where people and machines make things. You can work out this meaning from the words around it: «Smoke from vehicles, factories and burning rubbish pollutes the air.» Vehicles and burning rubbish both give off smoke, so factories must be places that give off smoke too.
explain: Writers sometimes compare one thing to another. «Put a pondweed leaf in a glass of water on a sunny windowsill and watch: tiny bubbles of oxygen rise from it, the factory working in front of your eyes.» A leaf is not a building, but it makes something, just as a factory does. So here factory means a place that makes things, and the writer uses it as a picture.
explain: Context also tells you about people's jobs. «Food-factory workers wear hairnets, white coats and gloves, wash their hands» shows that factory workers are careful, because they keep hair and germs out of the food. Always ask what word would fit, then check your meaning in the sentence.
fact: A factory is a big building where people and machines make things.
fact: Many things are made in a factory one after another on a line, called a production line.
fact: Factories began in the Industrial Revolution, and they can cause pollution.
fact: «Factory» is used in phrases such as factory worker, factory floor and made in a factory.
fact: In «Charlie and the Chocolate Factory», the factory is a place where chocolate is made.
exercise: What is a factory? | A shop where you buy toys | A farm where animals grow | A park with lots of machines | *A big building where people and machines make things
exercise: «Many things are made in a factory one after another on a line.» What is that line called? | A finishing line | A queue | A washing line | *A production line
exercise: «Food-factory workers wear hairnets, white coats and gloves.» Why do they wear hairnets? | To look fashionable | *So no hair gets into the food | To keep warm in winter | To stop the sun in their eyes
test: «Smoke from vehicles, factories and burning rubbish pollutes the air.» What does pollutes mean? | Warms | *Dirties or harms | Cleans | Lights up
test: «A factory robot is controlled by a computer.» What controls the robot? | The wind | A bell | *A computer | A fish in a tank
exam: «Put a pondweed leaf in a glass of water on a sunny windowsill and watch: tiny bubbles of oxygen rise from it, the factory working in front of your eyes.» What does the writer mean by the factory here? | A story about chocolate | A glass of water on a windowsill | A real building full of workers | *A place that makes something, like the leaf making oxygen bubbles
